  "notes": [
    {
      "title": "The problem before Cloudinary",
      "body": "The problem before\nAvatars sat in ./uploads on one Node box. The second server did not have the file. A container restart wiped the folder. Thumbnails meant ImageMagick on the API and a 4MB PNG in every img tag, as if each waiter stored a sack of film under the table instead of sending the negative to the lab.\n\nWhat this is\nCloudinary is a hosted photo lab plus a CDN. You upload once; the lab keeps the original bytes. You store a public_id in Mongo — locker number, not a print. The delivery URL is the order slip: cloud name, transforms such as w_400 or f_auto, then the id. The CDN caches each derived print at the edge so phones fetch a small copy without hitting your Node disk.\n\nWhat it solves\nBytes leave your Node disk and live in the lab. Any API replica can show the same picture from the ticket. A phone gets a small WebP from the URL; a desktop asks for a larger crop. You did not write two files or run ImageMagick. A restart no longer shreds the only copy under the table, because the negative sits in Cloudinary and the CDN still holds yesterday's prints.\n\nReal-life example\nThink of a print shop. You leave one negative: the original upload. The clerk prints a passport or a poster from that negative when the URL names the size. Your shop only keeps the ticket number (public_id) in the register. Nobody tapes film under every table. Next year you can still order an 8x10 from roll 42 without hunting a second envelope in the kitchen.\n\nUses\nUse Cloudinary for MERN avatars, product shots, and post images, and for take-homes that say do not store files in Mongo. Anywhere two Node boxes or a container restart would lose ./uploads, send the negative to the lab. The dining room stays stateless: the register notes public_id, the CDN hangs the print, and ImageMagick never runs under the API table during a list page of faces.\n\nWatch out\nNever put CLOUDINARY_API_SECRET in the React app or a VITE_ variable. That is handing the lab the master key to the safe. Anyone who opens DevTools can stamp fake tickets, shred Ada's negatives, or order huge prints on your bill. The browser may know cloud_name to build public URLs. Only the Node kitchen holds the secret, the same way JWT_SECRET never ships in the menu bundle."
    },
    {
      "title": "Upload, then transform in the URL",
      "body": "The problem before\nPeople saved five copies of every face: original, 400px, 80px, webp, and jpg. A new card size meant a new ImageMagick job and a migration of old files, as if the shop reprinted every negative by hand whenever the menu changed the frame. The register filled with five ticket numbers for one guest, and a sixth size still required another weekend of reprints.\n\nWhat this is\nOne upload stores the original in Cloudinary. Transforms are path segments on the delivery URL after /upload/: w_400, h_400, c_fill, f_auto, q_auto. That URL is the order slip, not a second file you saved. The public_id still names one negative. Cloudinary fetches or builds that derived print and the CDN caches it at the edge, the way a clerk prints a new size from the same drawer without you leaving a second roll on Node.\n\nWhat it solves\nThe React card asks for a square face crop. The hero asks for a wide banner. Both use the same public_id in Mongo. You did not store five files, and a new frame on the menu is a new order slip in the URL, not a weekend migration of every ticket. The CDN remembers each print so the second visitor does not wait, and Node never runs ImageMagick under the table.\n\nReal-life example\nThe ticket in the register says roll 42. You write passport or poster on the order slip, which is the transform string in the URL. The shop still has one negative in the drawer. Next year you can still order an 8x10 from that same ticket without hunting a second envelope. The clerk does not make you leave five rolls because the menu added a square frame.\n\nUses\nUse URL transforms for responsive images, avatars, and og:image tags. f_auto picks WebP or AVIF when the browser Accept header allows it. Reach for this whenever one negative must feed a tiny face crop, a wide card, and a social preview without five copies on disk. Build the src from public_id at render time so a new card size is a new slip, not a new upload job.\n\nWatch out\nDo not ship an unsigned preset that allows any transform from the browser. A stranger can make you pay for an 8000px render, like walking into the lab and ordering a billboard from someone else's ticket with no clerk to say no. Cap eager transforms and max sizes on the preset. Signed uploads from Node keep the expensive prints behind the staff door."
    },
    {
      "title": "Signed vs unsigned",
      "body": "The problem before\nThe upload widget sat in React with the api_secret. Anyone who opened DevTools could delete Ada's photos or upload a 200MB video to your bill. That is leaving the shop's master key on the public counter next to the guest book. A homework demo that needed one avatar turned into an open safe, and the lab would print whatever a stranger ordered from the sidewalk.\n\nWhat this is\nSigned upload: Node holds api_secret, signs the request, then Cloudinary accepts it. Unsigned upload: an upload preset you created in the dashboard, with folder and size rules, and no secret in the browser. The clerk either checks the manager stamp or follows the printed rule card on the drop box. public_id still names the locker; the CDN still serves the print; only the door into the lab changes.\n\nWhat it solves\nThe browser can still send a file. The lab only accepts what the preset or the signature allows. Guests never hold the master key, yet a homework drop box can still take a 2MB passport photo without a staff door. A real product stamps slips in the kitchen after login, so Ada cannot shred someone else's negative, and your bill does not pay for a stranger's 200MB video.\n\nReal-life example\nA locked staff door is signed upload: the manager stamps the slip with the safe combination. A drop box with a printed rule card is an unsigned preset: photos under 2MB, folder avatars, jpg or png only. Both get a negative into the lab. Only the stamp uses the combination. The drop box never hands guests the key; it only follows the card taped to the lid.\n\nUses\nHomework with no Node kitchen: an unsigned preset is fine if folder, max bytes, and formats are tight. A real product: sign on the server after login, or mint a short signature for the widget. Use signed for avatars in production. Keep unsigned only when the take-home has no Express door and the drop-box rules would make a clerk proud.\n\nWatch out\nAn unsigned preset with unsigned signing and no size cap is an open dumpster on your account. Strangers will fill the lab with 200MB video and eager billboard prints until the bill looks like a warehouse fire. Also never paste api_secret into the widget because a sample said so. The sample is a shop window; your secret is the safe behind the staff door."
    }
  ],
