    {
      "title": "Env and config (Node)",
      "lang": "javascript",
      "desc": "The problem before\ncloud_name lived in three files. The secret was in the repo. A teammate forked the kitchen and copied the safe combination. Laptop and prod pointed at different shop doors, so the waiter delivered photos to a lab that did not exist. One box uploaded; the other 404'd; DevTools later showed the combination because someone named it VITE_CLOUDINARY_API_SECRET for convenience.\n\nWhat this is\nThree values configure the SDK: cloud_name (public street name in every delivery URL), api_key (your account number), and api_secret (the safe combination that signs uploads and destroys). They live in .env on the server. Only the staff kitchen reads the last two at boot via cloudinary.config. React may learn cloud_name to build CDN URLs. It must never learn the secret. public_id is still what you store; the URL is still the print.\n\nWhat it solves\nOne config() at boot. React only needs the cloud name, never the secret. The dining room can still print from public CDN URLs, while the master key stays in the staff safe so a guest with DevTools cannot stamp tickets or shred lockers. Laptop and prod read the same three names from env, so you stop pointing waiters at a lab door that only exists on one machine.\n\nReal-life example\nThe shop's street name is public on the signboard. That is cloud_name in res.cloudinary.com URLs. The safe combination is not on the sign. You would not print the combination on the menu, and you would not check it into git next to the waiter's order pad. Render holds the three staff values the way the back office holds JWT_SECRET, not the way the window holds the hours of business.\n\nUses\nEvery Node upload and destroy. Call cloudinary.config once when Express boots, using Render or Vercel env for the API, the same way you load JWT_SECRET before the first guest walks into the dining room. If the UI must build delivery URLs, give it only the public cloud_name. Keep api_key and api_secret on the service that stamps tickets, never on the static frontend project.\n\nWatch out\nVITE_CLOUDINARY_API_SECRET is a trap. If it starts with VITE_ it ships to the browser. That is taping the safe combination to the shop window. Use VITE_ only for the public cloud_name if the UI must build delivery URLs. NEXT_PUBLIC_ is the same window. A leaked secret means rotate it in the dashboard, then treat every unsigned dump as already printed by a stranger.",
      "code": "// .env  (server only)\n// CLOUDINARY_CLOUD_NAME=demo\n// CLOUDINARY_API_KEY=123456\n// CLOUDINARY_API_SECRET=shhh\n\nconst cloudinary = require(\"cloudinary\").v2;\ncloudinary.config({\n  cloud_name: process.env.CLOUDINARY_CLOUD_NAME,\n  api_key: process.env.CLOUDINARY_API_KEY,\n  api_secret: process.env.CLOUDINARY_API_SECRET\n});"
    },
    {
      "title": "Upload from Express",
      "lang": "javascript",
      "desc": "The problem before\nmulter wrote ./uploads/ada.png. Box B did not have that path. A restart wiped the drawer. Two waiters in two dining rooms argued over a negative that only lived under one table. The API then returned a local path in JSON, so React asked box B for a file that never existed, and the card stayed broken until someone copied a folder by hand.\n\nWhat this is\ncloudinary.uploader.upload sends the temp file (path or buffer) to the lab. Cloudinary stores the original bytes, then returns public_id (locker number) and secure_url (one HTTPS print on the CDN). You save those in Mongo, not the bytes and not ./uploads/ada.png. The waiter carries the photo to the lab; the register only notes ticket 42. After that you may delete the multer slip so the dining room stays stateless.\n\nWhat it solves\nAny API replica can show the picture because the bytes are in the lab, not under table three. Restart does not wipe the negative. The dining room stays stateless, and a second Node box can still print from the same public_id without copying a folder between kitchens. React loads the CDN URL; Express never streams a 4000px file on every card request after the upload round trip.\n\nReal-life example\nThe waiter carries the photo to the print shop and comes back with ticket 42. The register only notes that number and maybe one secure_url snapshot. Nobody tapes the negative under table three. The next shift, or the other dining room across the street, still finds Ada's face from that ticket. The multer folder is a tray for today's slips, not the album the guests should see.\n\nUses\nPOST /avatar after login. Also POST /posts with a photo, any homework that will deploy to Render or Railway, and any take-home that says do not keep files on the API disk. Pair multer (or a buffer) with uploader.upload, then persist public_id. That is the pattern whenever two boxes or a sleeping container would otherwise lose the only copy of the face.\n\nWatch out\nDo not save the original filename as a disk path in Mongo. Trust public_id from Cloudinary. A path like ./uploads/ada.png is a dining-room drawer, and box B will 404 while the lab still holds the real negative. Also set folder and public_id on upload so the locker number is guessable later, not a random id that old tweets cannot find after a re-upload.",
      "code": "app.post(\"/avatar\", needLogin, upload.single(\"photo\"), async (req, res) => {\n  const out = await cloudinary.uploader.upload(req.file.path, {\n    folder: \"avatars\",\n    public_id: String(req.user.id),\n    overwrite: true\n  });\n  await User.updateOne(\n    { _id: req.user.id },\n    { avatarId: out.public_id, avatarUrl: out.secure_url }\n  );\n  res.json({ url: out.secure_url });\n});"
    },
    {
      "title": "Transform in the URL",
      "lang": "txt",
      "desc": "The problem before\nThe card needed 80px. The original was 4000px. The phone melted. Every list page downloaded the whole negative, as if the waiter dropped a poster on each table instead of a passport print. You had no second file, so you either shipped the camera PNG or ran ImageMagick on Node for every request, and both choices hurt the dining room.\n\nWhat this is\nInsert transforms after /upload/ and before the public_id: w_80, h_80, c_fill, f_auto, q_auto. That string is the order slip. Cloudinary builds that derived print from the original negative and the CDN caches it at the edge. You still store one public_id in Mongo. You did not save an 80px file. Changing the path orders a new size from the same locker without another upload.\n\nWhat it solves\nOne original. Many prints. The CDN caches each print so the second visitor is cheap. The React card can ask for a square face and the hero can ask for a wide crop without you storing two files or running ImageMagick on Node. A list of a hundred avatars can each be an 80px WebP instead of a 4000px PNG, and the phone bill and the lab bill both stay sane.\n\nReal-life example\nWrite passport, colour on the order slip. Same negative in the drawer. The clerk does not ask you to leave a second roll. Next week you can still write poster on a new slip and roll 42 is waiting. The URL is that slip: width, crop, format. The ticket number at the end is still avatars/ada. Friends who saved the ticket still find the locker; they just hang a different print.\n\nUses\nAvatars, cards, heroes, and og:image. Any page that would otherwise ship a 4000px PNG into an 80px hole. Prefer f_auto and q_auto so the clerk picks a format the frame can actually hold. Build these paths from public_id when you render, including email and Open Graph, so a new design size is a new slip instead of a new upload and a Mongo migration of old print URLs.\n\nWatch out\nHotlinking a raw /upload/v123/big.png in a list of 100 cards is hanging a poster on every table. Use a small fill crop in the URL, or the phone bill and your Cloudinary bill both melt. Also do not let an unsigned preset accept arbitrary huge w_ from the browser. A stranger ordering billboards from the drop box is how a demo account wakes up on fire.",
      "code": "# original\nhttps://res.cloudinary.com/demo/image/upload/v1/avatars/ada\n\n# 80×80 face crop, auto format + quality\nhttps://res.cloudinary.com/demo/image/upload/w_80,h_80,c_fill,g_face,f_auto,q_auto/avatars/ada\n\n# wide card\nhttps://res.cloudinary.com/demo/image/upload/w_800,c_fill,f_auto/posts/42"
    },
    {
      "title": "Build the URL in code",
      "lang": "javascript",
      "desc": "The problem before\nPeople concatenated strings and forgot a slash. The image 404'd. A missing comma in w_80h_80 produced a ticket the clerk could not read, and the card showed a broken frame all afternoon. Teams then copied twenty handmade URLs into Mongo, so a new 128px avatar meant a migration of expired slips instead of a new call at render time.\n\nWhat this is\ncloudinary.url(publicId, { width, height, crop, fetch_format: auto, quality: auto }) lets the SDK write a legal delivery URL. You still only store public_id — the locker number. The SDK fills the order slip: host, cloud name, transform segments, then the id. The CDN serves that print. You are not inventing the shop's street spelling, and you are not saving a second file for each size the menu might want.\n\nWhat it solves\nThe SDK writes a legal URL. You still only store public_id. A new card size is a new call at render time, not a migration of twenty saved print URLs sitting in Mongo like expired order slips. Typos in w_ and slashes go away. Server-rendered og:image and the React card share the same helper, so Ada's locker prints the right crop on every surface without five stored addresses.\n\nReal-life example\nThe clerk fills the order slip. You do not invent the shop's address format. You hand over ticket 42 and the print size; the lab already knows how to spell the street and the window in the URL. If the menu changes from 80px to 128px, you change the helper arguments, not the number in the register. The negative never left locker 42 while you argued about slashes.\n\nUses\nServer-rendered og:image. Emails. Any Node or React helper that must not typo a transform path. Build the delivery URL when you render, from the id you kept in the register. Use this whenever a card, a hero, and a social preview all need different w_ and crop from the same public_id, including take-homes that would otherwise paste a raw 4000px secure_url into every img tag.\n\nWatch out\nStoring twenty transform URLs in Mongo is the trap. Store the id. Build URLs when you render. A saved 80px URL is one print; next month the design wants 128px and you have no ticket left to order a new size if you only kept the print. Also pass secure true so the helper emits HTTPS. A mixed-content http print will break the card on a Vercel https page.",
      "code": "const url = cloudinary.url(\"avatars/ada\", {\n  width: 80,\n  height: 80,\n  crop: \"fill\",\n  gravity: \"face\",\n  fetch_format: \"auto\",\n  quality: \"auto\",\n  secure: true\n});\n// https://res.cloudinary.com/.../w_80,h_80,c_fill,g_face,f_auto,q_auto/avatars/ada"
    },
    {
      "title": "Unsigned preset (browser)",
      "lang": "javascript",
      "desc": "The problem before\nA homework app had no Node. They still needed a photo on a card. Teams then pasted the api_secret into React so the widget would work, which is leaving the master key on the guest drop box. DevTools showed the combination. A stranger could fill the lab with video or shred Ada's locker, and the demo bill looked like a warehouse fire by morning.\n\nWhat this is\nAn unsigned upload preset is a named rule card in the Cloudinary dashboard: signing mode unsigned, plus folder, max bytes, and allowed formats. The browser POSTs the file to api.cloudinary.com with cloud_name and the preset name. No api_secret. The lab still stores the original, returns public_id and secure_url, and the CDN serves later prints. The drop box already knows which paper types and sizes the clerk will accept without a manager stamp.\n\nWhat it solves\nA tiny demo can upload without an Express kitchen. You cap size and folder on the preset so the dumpster stays closed. Guests never see the safe combination, yet a 2MB jpg can still land in avatars. React then stores public_id or the returned URL the same way a signed upload would. When the homework becomes a product, you move the stamp into Node without changing what the register keeps.\n\nReal-life example\nA drop box labeled avatars, 2MB, jpg and png only. The guest drops the photo. The clerk follows the printed card. There is no manager stamp and no master key on the sidewalk, only the rule card taped to the box. The register still gets ticket 42. The CDN still hangs the print. The difference is the door: a public slot with rules, not a staff corridor with the combination.\n\nUses\nStudent projects, the Cloudinary Upload Widget, and take-homes with no Express kitchen. Keep the preset tight: folder, max size, allowed formats, no open-ended eager billboards. Move to signed uploads the day the homework becomes a product with real users and a real bill. Until then the unsigned name is the only string the browser should know besides the public cloud_name.\n\nWatch out\nShipping the api_secret because the widget docs showed it is the classic fail. Use the unsigned preset name only. The docs sample is a shop window; your secret is the safe. If the preset has no size cap, the dumpster is open. Also do not let the guest pick public_id from a form field, or they will overwrite someone else's locker while the clerk follows the card and says yes.",
      "code": "// dashboard: Settings → Upload → Add upload preset\n// Signing mode: Unsigned\n// Folder: avatars   Max: 2 MB   Formats: jpg, png, webp\n\nconst form = new FormData();\nform.append(\"file\", fileInput.files[0]);\nform.append(\"upload_preset\", \"unsigned_avatars\");\n\nconst res = await fetch(\n  \"https://api.cloudinary.com/v1_1/YOUR_CLOUD/image/upload\",\n  { method: \"POST\", body: form }\n);\nconst data = await res.json();\n// data.public_id   data.secure_url"
    },
    {
      "title": "Delete (destroy)",
      "lang": "javascript",
      "desc": "The problem before\nAda changed her photo. The old 4000px file stayed forever. The bill grew. After she deleted the account, the album was still in the shop window, and the lab kept printing from a cancelled ticket. Mongo said the field was empty, but the CDN still had the poster, and a leaked URL still showed her face to anyone who had saved the old print.\n\nWhat this is\nuploader.destroy(public_id) tells Cloudinary to remove that asset and its derived versions from the lab. Then you clear the id and any saved URL in Mongo. The clerk shreds the negative and leftover prints, so the ticket no longer hangs on the public board. public_id is the locker you empty. The register must not keep a number for a roll that already went into the shredder.\n\nWhat it solves\nWhen the user deletes the post, the bytes go too. Replace and account-wipe stay in sync with the lab. You do not keep paying for posters of a guest who already left, and a GDPR-style takedown has a real shredder instead of an empty Mongo field in front of a still-public CDN print. A new avatar can overwrite locker 42; the old face should not remain in the shop window.\n\nReal-life example\nThe ticket is cancelled. The shop shreds the negative. A new face is a new ticket or the same locker with overwrite. The register must not keep a number for a roll that already went into the shredder. If Ada's friends still have an old URL, you at least stopped the lab from treating that locker as live. The waiter checks the name on the ticket before the clerk starts the machine.\n\nUses\nAccount delete, replace avatar, admin takedown. Any flow where the user says remove my photo, or the product must not leave old faces in the shop window after a new upload. Call destroy from the logged-in API after you load the owner's public_id from Mongo. Then blank the fields. That pairing is what interviews mean by staying in sync with object storage, not only with the user document.\n\nWatch out\nNever destroy from the browser with the api_secret. Do it on the server after you check the owner. A guest with the master key can shred someone else's locker. The waiter checks the name on the ticket first. Also do not destroy a guessed public_id from the request body; load avatarId from the logged-in user row, or you will empty the wrong drawer because a form field lied.",
      "code": "app.delete(\"/avatar\", needLogin, async (req, res) => {\n  const user = await User.findById(req.user.id);\n  if (user.avatarId) {\n    await cloudinary.uploader.destroy(user.avatarId);\n  }\n  user.avatarId = \"\";\n  user.avatarUrl = \"\";\n  await user.save();\n  res.json({ ok: true });\n});"
    }
