      "level": "intermediate",
      "q": "Can anyone see a Cloudinary URL?",
      "a": "The problem before\nPeople thought a long URL was a secret. They put medical photos in an unguessable folder. A shop-window print is still a shop-window print, even if the street is long and the locker number looks random. A leaked email, a sitemap, or a guessed avatars/ada path fetched the file. They had stored public delivery URLs in Mongo and called it private because the folder said kyc.\n\nWhat this is\nA normal Cloudinary delivery URL is fetchable by anyone who has it or who guesses the public_id. The CDN will serve the print. public_id is a locker name, not a password. For private assets you use authenticated or signed URLs with expiry, or a private CDN setting. Public avatars hang in the window on purpose. X-rays stay in a locked drawer. Cropping a public original does not add a lock.\n\nWhat it solves\nPublic avatars: fine, and that is why img src works without a JWT. Sensitive files: signed expiry, or S3 with a presign. You can still show a timed pass for an X-ray without pretending a long folder name is a lock. Interviews want you to separate the shop window from the locked drawer. Cloudinary can do both; the default upload is the window. Choose signed URLs when the product is KYC.\n\nReal-life example\nA shop window print is public. An X-ray stays in a locked drawer and you show a timed pass. Nobody should hang KYC photos in the window just because the locker said /kyc/ada and looked hard to guess. A guest who saw the URL, a leaked sitemap, or a guessed ticket still walks up to the glass. The clerk will hand over the print unless you asked for authenticated delivery and a signature that expires.\n\nUses\nSocial avatars versus KYC documents. Profile faces can use public delivery and f_auto crops. Passports, medical shots, and ID scans need signed URLs, short expiry, or a private bucket, not an unsigned public folder. Use this split in internships when they ask if Cloudinary is secure. Say: public media is public; secrets stay in env; private media needs a different delivery type, not a clever path.\n\nWatch out\nKYC images on a public unsigned folder named /kyc/ada.jpg is the trap. The path is not a password. Anyone with the URL, a guess, or a leaked sitemap can fetch the print from the shop window. Do not rely on security through obscurity. Also do not put those files in an unsigned preset. If the product cannot allow a public window, use signed URLs or keep the bytes in a private warehouse with presigns.",
