      "level": "beginner",
      "q": "How do you explain Cloudinary in an interview?",
      "a": "The problem before\nCandidates said it is for images and froze. They had no shop tour: no negative, no ticket, no passport print, no master key. The interviewer waited for a 90-second story and got a homepage slogan. Follow-ups about ./uploads, Mongo base64, and VITE_ secrets then went quiet. The candidate could not say what lives in the database versus what lives on the CDN, so the round felt like a brochure, not a shift they had worked.\n\nWhat this is\nOne minute: we do not keep bytes on the API disk. We upload to Cloudinary, store public_id in Mongo, and put w_ h_ f_auto in the delivery URL so the CDN prints the size we need from one negative. api_secret stays on Node. Unsigned preset is the homework shortcut when there is no kitchen stamp. public_id is the locker; the URL is the order slip; the lab plus CDN hang the prints.\n\nWhat it solves\nYou sound like you have shipped an avatar, not only read the homepage. They hear disk versus lab, ticket versus print, and why the secret never sat in React. That is the difference between a slogan and a shift you have worked. You can then answer signed versus unsigned, S3 versus the lab, and destroy on account wipe without starting over. The 90 seconds bought you the rest of the round.\n\nReal-life example\nNinety-second shop tour: negative, ticket, passport print, master key stays with the manager. Walk multer, upload, save public_id, img with w_80, then stop. Do not tour every crop flag unless they ask for the poster. If they ask why not S3, say warehouse versus lab. If they ask about secrets, say the combination is in the kitchen env, not VITE_. End on overwrite or destroy so they know the locker has a shredder.\n\nUses\nMERN intern and SDE-1 screens. Amazon, TCS, and campus labs all ask avatar upload. Keep this tour ready, then offer signed versus unsigned if they still have time left in the round. Use the same story in take-home READMEs: stateless API, public_id in Mongo, transforms in the URL, secret on Render. It also answers why not ./uploads on a container host without a five-minute detour into storage theory.\n\nWatch out\nA ten-minute tour of every crop flag is how you lose the room. They asked for avatar upload. If you start listing gravity and dpr, they will cut you and ask where the secret lives. Finish the five-step kitchen first. Do not say Cloudinary replaces the database. Do not invent private security from a long URL. And do not demo the widget with api_secret in the snippet you paste into the shared editor.",
